  "text": "The dog is a symbolic animal in Celtic mythology. Dogs appear often in the myths and folklore of Celtic lands. Some appear as the animal form of a divinity, usually a goddess. Others appear as companions to heroes, usually male. Still others are spirit-beings tied to the Otherworld. The link between dogs and religion may be very old. Bone remains at early sites suggest dogs were killed sacrificially. Many Celtic peoples still dislike eating dog meat. This disgust may recall a taboo on eating the flesh of a sacrificial victim. The goddess Nehalennia appears with a small lapdog at her feet. Fruit and eggs on her altars link the dog to fertility and abundance. The healing goddess Sirona also appears with a dog. Dogs lick their wounds until they heal. This may be why Celtic people believed dogs could heal human wounds through licking. Not all dogs are healers. The dog on the Gundestrup Cauldron seems tied to death, as is the folkloric Black Dog. In the Celtic view death led to rebirth, so dog images in graves may promise future life. Dogs and corn appear together on statues, linking seed death to new growth. The goddess Bóand drowned with her lapdog Dabilla. Lí Ban became a mermaid with her pet dog. The hero-warrior link is strong. The word cú, or hound, opens the names Cúchulainn and Cú Roí. Heroes kept hunting hounds such as Adhnúall, Bran, and Sceolan, dogs of Fionn mac Cumhaill. The Welsh Drudwyn served the hero Kulhwch. Failinis was the hound of the god Lugh. Several gods were tied to dogs, notably Nodens. Their hunting embodied the life-and-death cycle. The Black Dog was an apparition with burning eyes and a terrible howl. It warned of death and war across Ireland, Britain, and Germany. Irish belief tied such dogs to the banshee. Dogs howling near a sick person were said to predict death. The Cú Síth, or fairy dog, had a dark green coat. It moved soundlessly in a straight line. It could bark loudly, but only three times. On the third bark it sprang forward and devoured anyone near. On Lewis in the Hebrides, a survivor could pull a tooth from the Cú Síth, which served as an oracle. Even ordinary dogs in Scotland kept some power. A dog howling at the moon or growling at nothing was warning of fairy presences. Dogs were believed to see ghosts, witches, and other beings hidden to people without second sight.",
